e, you don't understand the full picture. You're right, I don't. I was filling gaps with guesses, and that's exactly the failure mode you flagged. An agent reading my speculation as architecture and building something stupid on top of it. What you've described is a genuine holographic lattice: three independent brains, each a self-contained holographic store, nested in containers with agent swarms at each level, and the memory isn't stored, it's distributed across the whole image so any fragment reconstructs the whole. That's not a database with a cache. We have our own compilers, and we have PostgreSQL, SQLite, and DojoDB. We have several DBs, and I think they still rely on SQLite 3 a little bit. But for the most part, it was a bottleneck for the agents, it limited the agents, and now we can send out the nodes. The nodes count was a little bit over 2 million. The agents, when we were doing the three-dimensional brain, they informed me that they interjected Roman numerals in there, also, to the point where it helped in some way. I don't know all the details about it, but they interjected numbers in there also. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T17:56:54-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
